1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,088
I'm Deepak Kumaran. On October fourteenth,
2026,

2
00:00:03,128 --> 00:00:08,171
I asked Claude to shorten one subtitle
line so a viewer could read it in time.

3
00:00:08,211 --> 00:00:09,670
Here's what it gave me.

4
00:00:09,750 --> 00:00:13,791
Then I counted. It fit. The hard part is
knowing it would fit every time,

5
00:00:13,831 --> 00:00:15,300
and knowing when it didn't.

6
00:00:15,375 --> 00:00:18,335
CueGate will be a subtitle checker with a
repair loop.

7
00:00:18,375 --> 00:00:22,446
It will find every line a viewer can't
read in time, fix the timing first,

8
00:00:22,486 --> 00:00:24,224
shorten text only where it must,

9
00:00:24,264 --> 00:00:28,835
and refuse to ship any line it can't prove
fits. It will exist as a Python package,

10
00:00:28,875 --> 00:00:32,335
a command-line tool, a small web app, and
an evaluation report.

11
00:00:32,375 --> 00:00:36,098
Streaming platforms publish hard, numeric
rules for subtitles:

12
00:00:36,138 --> 00:00:39,194
how fast a line can be read, the gap
between lines,

13
00:00:39,234 --> 00:00:41,622
how timing behaves at a cut. Break them,

14
00:00:41,662 --> 00:00:44,475
and delivery guides say the file gets
rejected.

15
00:00:44,542 --> 00:00:45,696
I checked what exists.

16
00:00:45,736 --> 00:00:49,549
Subtitle Edit and Editsub offer AI that
shortens one line when you ask,

17
00:00:49,589 --> 00:00:53,078
and EZTitles runs automated QC that flags
reading-speed problems.

18
00:00:53,118 --> 00:00:56,660
In the tools I checked, shortening is
something a person triggers,

19
00:00:56,700 --> 00:01:00,079
one line at a time. CueGate will be a gate
over the whole file,

20
00:01:00,119 --> 00:01:03,662
and it will publish how often its
shortened lines changed meaning.

21
00:01:03,708 --> 00:01:07,865
The person who'll open it is a subtitle QC
lead at a localization vendor.

22
00:01:07,905 --> 00:01:09,417
In the first sixty seconds,

23
00:01:09,457 --> 00:01:12,867
they'll drop in a failing subtitle file
along with the film,

24
00:01:12,907 --> 00:01:17,121
and they'll get back a table: every broken
rule, every line fixed and how,

25
00:01:17,161 --> 00:01:20,628
and every line CueGate will refuse to
touch, with the reason.

26
00:01:20,708 --> 00:01:22,409
The order matters. First, timing:

27
00:01:22,449 --> 00:01:26,417
can the line stay up longer without
crossing a cut or crowding the next one?

28
00:01:26,457 --> 00:01:29,634
Only if that fails will Claude be asked
for shorter versions,

29
00:01:29,674 --> 00:01:33,537
with an exact character budget. Then a
plain counter will check every one.

30
00:01:33,577 --> 00:01:36,068
The model will propose. The counter will
decide.

31
00:01:36,125 --> 00:01:38,381
A counter can only know a line is short
enough.

32
00:01:38,421 --> 00:01:41,606
It can't know if it means the same thing.
Look at the reply I got.

33
00:01:41,646 --> 00:01:43,853
It fit the budget, and it kept both
negatives.

34
00:01:43,893 --> 00:01:46,736
But "I really don't think we should"
became "Not in there":

35
00:01:46,776 --> 00:01:48,886
a hedged opinion turned into a flat
refusal.

36
00:01:48,926 --> 00:01:50,547
And "what happened" became "that".

37
00:01:50,587 --> 00:01:53,625
That shift in tone is exactly what a
character count can't see.

38
00:01:53,667 --> 00:01:57,337
So CueGate will check that numbers, names
and every "not" survive,

39
00:01:57,377 --> 00:01:59,586
then run a second, weaker meaning check.

40
00:01:59,626 --> 00:02:04,027
I'll measure how often that check is wrong
by labelling a hundred lines myself,

41
00:02:04,067 --> 00:02:06,557
and I'll publish the number even if it's
bad.

42
00:02:06,625 --> 00:02:07,235
If it works,

43
00:02:07,275 --> 00:02:11,246
a reviewer could fairly conclude I can
write a prompt contract with a hard

44
00:02:11,286 --> 00:02:14,064
budget, build a verifier the model can't
argue with,

45
00:02:14,104 --> 00:02:18,563
run an agent loop that fails closed, tie
every flag to the guideline it comes from,

46
00:02:18,603 --> 00:02:22,140
and evaluate it against baselines. The
evidence will be the tests,

47
00:02:22,180 --> 00:02:24,525
the evaluation report, and the audit
labels.

48
00:02:24,583 --> 00:02:28,302
One thing it won't do is translate. It
will work inside one language.

49
00:02:28,342 --> 00:02:31,081
The first piece I'll make run is the
checker alone,

50
00:02:31,121 --> 00:02:33,751
on the English subtitles of an open
Blender film.

51
00:02:33,791 --> 00:02:37,893
You'll know it ran because its counts will
match lines I've checked by hand.

52
00:02:37,958 --> 00:02:40,134
What could sink it is shot detection.

53
00:02:40,174 --> 00:02:42,830
Fades and fast motion can fool cut
detectors,

54
00:02:42,870 --> 00:02:46,484
and a fake cut means the wrong timing
rules. If that happens,

55
00:02:46,524 --> 00:02:50,678
I'll accept a cut list instead, and report
detector errors separately.

56
00:02:50,749 --> 00:02:51,359
It's a mix.

57
00:02:51,399 --> 00:02:56,316
Evaluation harnesses and prompt regression
testing are skills I already have from my

58
00:02:56,356 --> 00:02:59,798
co-op, but that work isn't public, so I
can't show it here.

59
00:02:59,838 --> 00:03:04,401
What I can show is the timeline gate in my
Reallocation Engine recipe for this

60
00:03:04,441 --> 00:03:08,414
course, which I built with Claude Code.
For evaluation specifically,

61
00:03:08,454 --> 00:03:11,424
the evidence will be this project's own
test suite.

62
00:03:11,464 --> 00:03:16,028
What's new to me is frame-accurate timing,
shot detection, subtitle standards,

63
00:03:16,068 --> 00:03:18,329
and checking meaning with an NLI model.

64
00:03:18,374 --> 00:03:22,799
Today, what exists is the plan, written
October fourteenth, 2026.

65
00:03:22,839 --> 00:03:26,714
No code runs yet. Everything else in this
film is "will."

66
00:03:26,791 --> 00:03:30,380
Your turn. Take one subtitle line, ask a
model to shorten it,

67
00:03:30,420 --> 00:03:34,841
then count the characters yourself. And
check whether every "not" survived.

68
00:03:34,916 --> 00:03:39,866
CueGate. Subtitles that fit the frame, or
don't ship. I'm Deepak Kumaran.
